/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  lle01_acetic_mibk_perry
  =======================

  WHAT IT IS.  The first VALIDATION READING of the liquid-liquid flash that
  the `extractor` runs at every stage: the catalogue's UNIFAC, with no
  parameter touched, laid beside the six measured tie lines of water +
  acetic acid + methyl isobutyl ketone (MIBK) at 25 C, 1 atm
  (constant/experiments: Sherwood, Evans & Longcor 1939, as printed in
  Perry's Chemical Engineers' Handbook 9th ed., Table 15-8, p. 15-48).

  HOW IT READS.  `lleTieLines` feeds the flash along a straight line in the
  triangle (acid mass fraction 0.005 -> 0.40, the rest water : MIBK 1 : 1),
  and writes the conjugate pair (raffinate, extract) of every feed that
  splits, in MASS fractions.  The overlay interpolates the model's extract
  composition at each MEASURED raffinate acid fraction (the abscissa) and
  reports the average absolute deviation per column.  Nothing is fitted:
  this is a reading, pinned by `aad` rows, not a calibration.

  WHAT IT FOUND (mass fractions, AAD over the six tie lines):

      raffinate water     0.036      extract acetic acid  0.031
      raffinate MIBK      0.036      extract water        0.043
                                     extract MIBK         0.056

  UNIFAC DOES split the system and has the right shape of the binodal, but
  it MISSES the measurements by 3 to 6 mass percent -- a miss, and the
  verdict word the gate holds is `misses` (band declared here, a priori and
  for every column: AAD <= 0.01 `agrees`, <= 0.03 `approximate`, above
  `misses`).  The misses have a direction: the model puts too much acetic
  acid in the MIBK (extract acid 0.130 against the measured 0.089 at the
  raffinate's 0.117, and 0.222 against 0.173 at 0.205), dissolves about twice
  the measured water in the dilute extract (0.088 against 0.054 at 0.117),
  and keeps the two liquids apart up to a raffinate acid fraction of about
  0.395, past the last measured tie line (0.346, where the measured extract
  is still 0.31 water).  This is the corpus UNIFAC, the VLE parameter table
  (CLAUDE.md sec. 6: the same table keeps water / 1-butanol miscible); a
  curated LLE parameter set, or NRTL pairs fitted on a held-out design, is
  the remedy and is NOT done here.  Consequence for a student: a countercurrent
  cascade on this package (tutorials/steady/absorption/extract03_acetic_acid_mibk_perry) takes
  more acid out per stage than the real system does.

  NOT IN THE READING.  The all-binary row of Table 15-8 (water / MIBK mutual
  solubility) is not carried; the data are transcribed from the table's
  page image and NOT checked against the 1939 article; the MIBK vapour
  pressure advisory in the log is about a property the LLE never uses.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
operations
(
    {
        name   tie_unifac;
        type   lleTieLines;
        state  { T 25 degC;  P 1 atm; }
        solute aceticAcid;  carrier water;  extractant methylIsobutylKetone;
        feed
        {
            soluteMassFraction { from 0.005;  to 0.40;  n 80; }
            carrierToExtractant 1.0;
        }
        output { file tie_unifac.csv; }
    }
);

experimental
(
    {
        name      perry_table15_8;
        kind      lle;
        models    ( tie_unifac );
        dataset   "constant/experiments/perry_table15_8_sherwood1939.dat";
        source    "Sherwood, Evans & Longcor 1939 via Perry 9th ed. Table 15-8";
    }
);
